Windows native validation follow-up for milestone 3: coordinated shutdown receipt cleanup.

Observed native failure on the installed f6b4e73d build:
- Beaver automatically launched its owned Ollama on the second and subsequent cold starts.
- Closing Beaver through the real Windows close button stopped every Beaver and Ollama process, but ollama-process-receipt.json remained.
- The failure reproduced twice. A dedicated process watcher observed Beaver helpers changing at 21.109 s, owned Ollama gone at 24.642 s, Beaver gone at 24.748 s, while the receipt remained.

Root cause:
- Cleanup intentionally stops Ollama after all client services.
- The manager received ollama_setup_deadline (origin + 3 s) for both operation-lock admission and final process reap.
- By the time preceding services completed, that short deadline had expired. The idle owned daemon was not reaped by the manager; process::exit then skipped Rust destructors, while Windows ownership still killed the child and left the durable receipt.

Decision:
- Keep the 3-second absolute deadline as the sole bound for waiting on a concurrent Ollama setup/update operation.
- Give an already-idle owned process the remaining global graceful deadline for terminate, reap, receipt removal and emergency-registration release.
- A late shutdown first uses try_lock: an idle manager can still clean up; a genuinely active operation remains fail-closed after its setup deadline.
- The stop authority was split into manager_stop.rs so every production source file remains below 230 lines.

